The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (!futex_requeue_pi_prepare(top_waiter, NULL))
    return -EAGAIN;
and
plist_del(&q->list, &hb->chain);
futex_hb_waiters_dec(hb);
Reason:
When the waiter (Task A) leaves early due to signal/timeout, it sets `requeue_state` to `Q_REQUEUE_PI_IGNORE` inside `futex_requeue_pi_prepare()`. The requeuer (Task B) calls that function, sees the failure, and returns `-EAGAIN` without removing the waiter from the list. Task B then retries indefinitely because the same top waiter remains in the list, while Task A cannot remove itself because it is blocked waiting for the hash bucket lock held by Task B. The unconditional removal in the wakeup path also fails to handle the case where the requeuer might concurrently remove the waiter, causing a double removal.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!futex_requeue_pi_prepare(top_waiter, NULL)) {
    plist_del(&top_waiter->list, &hb1->chain);
    futex_hb_waiters_dec(hb1);
    return -EAGAIN;
}
and
if (!plist_node_empty(&q->list)) {
    plist_del(&q->list, &hb->chain);
    futex_hb_waiters_dec(hb);
}
Reason:
By removing the top waiter from the list immediately after the `futex_requeue_pi_prepare()` failure, the requeuer makes progress to the next waiter (if any) instead of endlessly retrying the same stale waiter. This breaks the livelock. The conditional removal in the wakeup path (`handle_early_requeue_pi_wakeup`) ensures that the waiter only removes itself from the list if it has not already been removed by the requeuer, preventing a double free. The two changes together synchronize the removal and avoid the race.

